  "scenario": "MPLS L2VPN EoMPLS Multi-Service Aggregation",
  "goals": "• {{router3_name}} and {{router4_name}} are two provider edge routers joined by the single MPLS core link {{link3_cidr}}, where OSPF area 0, the Loopback0 addresses and LDP are already configured and operational.\n• Four customer routers hang off them over layer 2 attachment circuits whose PE side has no IP address, and none of them runs a routing protocol. They belong to two different customers who happen to use the same address range {{service_cidr}}:\n    service A: {{router1_name}} ({{service_ip1}}) on {{router3_name}} and {{router5_name}} ({{service_ip2}}) on {{router4_name}}\n    service B: {{router2_name}} ({{service_ip3}}) on {{router3_name}} and {{router6_name}} ({{service_ip4}}) on {{router4_name}}\n• Carry both services as Ethernet over MPLS pseudowires in port mode over the same pair of PEs, keeping them strictly separate:\n    service A uses virtual circuit identifier {{vc_id_a}}\n    service B uses virtual circuit identifier {{vc_id_b}}\n• Keep the pseudowire settings in one reusable pseudowire class named {{pw_class}} on both PEs, using MPLS encapsulation, and use that class for both services instead of writing the encapsulation on each cross connect.\n• Every customer-facing interface must be bound to exactly one pseudowire, and each PE must end up with exactly two virtual circuits.\n• Result: {{router1_name}} must reach {{service_ip2}} and {{router2_name}} must reach {{service_ip4}}, while the two services stay isolated: {{router1_name}} must not be able to reach {{service_ip3}} or {{service_ip4}} even though they are in the same subnet.",
